Limit: only a refused or unanswered GitHub call is resumable by retrying; a wrong target or a drifted resource stops the operation for a person
Ruled-out: rolling back GitHub writes after a partial failure | deleting a repository needs a broader credential (delete_repo) than creating it, a public repository is observable once it exists, and a rollback can itself fail halfway; the ledger keeps exact receipts and a retry resumes instead
Ruled-out: GitHub calls made directly inside the producer | a refusal is only shown to write nothing if every write passes through one countable port, and this packet may not write to GitHub, so the production path is exercised only through injected seams
Ruled-out: receipts that restate the plan's values | a receipt carries what GitHub answered in a read after the write; a copied plan value would only show that the plan agrees with itself
Ruled-out: pushing a new commit when a push is already receipted | the new commit's timestamp differs from the one GitHub holds, so the push step fetches the receipted commit and resets onto it
Warn: keep the GitHub receipt ledger beside the checkout, never inside it: a failed run removes the checkout, and the ledger is what a retry resumes from
Unverified: the production gh port has never run against GitHub; it is exercised only through an injected client and git runner
